Now to tell you more about how
you can leverage AI and ML,

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:40.030
I'd like to invite my
colleague, Dale, onto the stage.

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:41.382
Please give it up for Dale.

00:11:41.382 --> 00:11:44.120
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:45.828
DALE MARKOWITZ: Thanks, Seth.

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:49.020
Hi, I'm Dale.

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:51.540
I'm a developer for
AI and Google Cloud.

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:53.070
So let's talk about
how we can add

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:56.850
a little intelligence into our
app to make it easier to use.

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:59.380
And why might we
want to do that?

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:02.970
Well, you saw Seth's nice
Flutter app for ordering food.

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:04.560
But let's say that
you're a restaurant

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:07.623
and you want to get
listed on our app.

00:12:07.623 --> 00:12:09.510
Oops.

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:14.700
Then you probably have a
menu that you need to upload.

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:17.000
And your menu is
probably analog,

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:20.430
so you need to have somebody
manually type in all

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:23.460
of the foods and prices, and
all of this metadata to become

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:25.987
listed in the gEats tool.

00:12:25.987 --> 00:12:27.570
And that's really
annoying, especially

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:29.380
if you're trying to
do this on mobile.

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:34.430
So can AI help make that
part of our app easier?

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:35.340
Let me show you how.

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:36.935
Let's hop to the phone.

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:47.320
I'm clearly a pixel user.

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:48.910
All right, cool.

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:51.070
So you saw this nice
app that Seth built.

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:54.160
But let's look at the admin side
that a restaurant owner would

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:55.090
use.

00:12:55.090 --> 00:12:56.530
As you can see in
this interface,

00:12:56.530 --> 00:12:59.590
there's no text field for
writing foods or prices.

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:04.730
Instead, we just upload
a photo of our menu.

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:07.580
That photo gets uploaded
to the cloud and analyzed.

00:13:07.580 --> 00:13:09.450
And that analysis can
take a few seconds.

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:13.560
So let me show you the
picture that I just uploaded.

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:15.780
It's this menu I was
holding on stage.

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:18.030
And as you can see, there's
lots of useful information

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:21.990
here, like the name of our cafe,
its address and phone number,

00:13:21.990 --> 00:13:23.730
plus all of this
food information,

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:27.360
like the headings, salads,
mains, the food items

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:30.170
and their prices.

00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:32.680
So let's hop back to the phone.

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:33.970
And, ah, look at that.

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:35.890
We got lots of
information from our menu

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:37.750
without even having
to type it in.

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:39.230
Let's take a look at it.

00:13:39.230 --> 00:13:42.310
So we identified Googleplex
Cafe, its phone number

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:43.400
and address.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:46.330
We also got these nice
little ratings stars,

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:49.370
and a map showing where
our restaurant's located,

00:13:49.370 --> 00:13:51.850
and this cool user-generated
picture of the Google

00:13:51.850 --> 00:13:53.470
headquarters.

00:13:53.470 --> 00:13:55.960
We also did a pretty good
job of parsing the foods

00:13:55.960 --> 00:14:00.280
on the menu, the descriptions--
veggie burger on a pretzel bun.

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:04.330
I also want to point out that
this part wasn't 100% perfect.

00:14:04.330 --> 00:14:06.310
We didn't identify the
price of the chili,

00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:09.430
or we thought that
cherry-lime was a food and not

00:14:09.430 --> 00:14:10.870
a type of gelato.

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:12.710
But we still saved
a lot of time.

00:14:12.710 --> 00:14:16.880
And we can always go in and
edit those specific details.

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:18.340
So how did we make
that possible?

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:19.630
Let's head back to the slides.

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:26.890
So let's just recap
what just happened.

00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:29.230
First, I uploaded a photo.

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:33.790
Then we took the photo
and extract it into text.

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:35.950
Then we determined the
location, the rating,

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:37.780
and we pulled some photos.

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:40.270
And then we also parsed
the menu itself, the foods

00:14:40.270 --> 00:14:41.570
and their prices.

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:43.540
So how did we do it?

00:14:43.540 --> 00:14:46.460
To get the text, we used
the Google Vision API.

00:14:46.460 --> 00:14:48.610
And once we had our
menu in text format,

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:51.940
we could pass it to the
Google Natural Language API.

00:14:51.940 --> 00:14:54.070
This tool has lots of
useful functionality

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:55.510
for analyzing text.

00:14:55.510 --> 00:14:59.090
But we just focused on the
entity extraction feature,

00:14:59.090 --> 00:15:01.300
which finds substrings
in our menu text

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:06.010
that correspond to prices, phone
numbers, addresses, and more.

00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:08.170
And once we extracted
that phone number,

00:15:08.170 --> 00:15:10.630
that was very useful
because we could use it

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:13.820
to query the Google Places API.

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:15.800
So Google will take
a phone number,

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:18.340
and if it recognizes that
that phone number corresponds

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:21.010
to a place, it can return
you useful metadata

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:24.790
like the star rating
of the restaurant,

00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:29.710
user-generated photos,
location, and more.

00:15:29.710 --> 00:15:32.370
But actually, the most difficult
part about building this app

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:35.640
was identifying those
menu-specific entities

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:38.310
like that "grilled
cheese" was a food,

00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:41.910
and that "on a pretzel bun"
was a description of a food.

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:45.180
Because that's so specific
to our context of menu

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:48.240
parsing that there's nothing
in the box in a Google API

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:50.550
that recognizes these terms.

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:52.810
For that, we had to
build a custom model.

00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:57.570
And we did that using a tool
called Google Cloud AutoML.

00:15:57.570 --> 00:16:01.920
The way that works is you upload
a labeled training data set.

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:03.990
AutoML builds a model
for you, and then it

00:16:03.990 --> 00:16:06.150
hosts your model in
a REST API that you

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:08.033
can call to get predictions.

00:16:08.033 --> 00:16:10.450
But rather than explain this,
let me just show you a demo.

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:11.640
Let's hop to the laptop.

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:21.260
So here I am in the Google
Cloud Platform console.

00:16:21.260 --> 00:16:24.440
And I'm in the Natural
Language AI tool space.

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:27.687
And there are lots of
different types of text models

00:16:27.687 --> 00:16:28.520
that we could build.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:31.460
But we're going to build
an entity extraction model.

00:16:31.460 --> 00:16:34.950
That's for identifying
inline text items.

00:16:34.950 --> 00:16:38.010
So our first step is to find
a data set and upload it.

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:42.510
And I tried a few, and
this one worked the best.

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:44.260
And so what happened
was I found this data

00:16:44.260 --> 00:16:45.960
set provided by
the New York Public

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:49.380
Library of pictures
of historical menus

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:51.420
that had been uploaded.

00:16:51.420 --> 00:16:54.790
And I used the Vision API to
extract the text from them.

00:16:54.790 --> 00:16:56.910
So let me show you
what that looked like.

00:16:56.910 --> 00:17:00.060
Easy Beef, a
restaurant in Virginia.

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:02.460
And then I went in
and I hand-labeled

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:04.710
all of the entities
that I cared about.

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:08.250
I just did that by clicking and
dragging, and then saying, ah,

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:11.410
a cheeseburger is a food.

00:17:11.410 --> 00:17:14.470
And after I did this
for about two hours,

00:17:14.470 --> 00:17:17.300
I got really bored
of labeling data.

00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:19.720
This is the very boring part
of building machine learning

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:20.589
models.

00:17:20.589 --> 00:17:23.470
But luckily, Google now offers
a data labeling service,

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:25.130
where if you want a
data set like this

00:17:25.130 --> 00:17:27.609
you can send it to
the labeling service.

00:17:27.609 --> 00:17:31.790
Google will label your data,
and then you'll be good to go.

00:17:31.790 --> 00:17:33.760
One thing I'll also
point out is that I

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:36.400
didn't have a ton of menus to
work with in that data set,

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:38.080
only under 200.

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:40.270
And ideally, when you're
building a model like this,

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:42.880
you really want more like 1,000.

00:17:42.880 --> 00:17:45.410
But as people use our app
we'll collect more data.

00:17:45.410 --> 00:17:48.480
So that's fine for now.

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:50.040
Now we want to
train a model to be

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:53.170
able to do that annotation
for us automatically.

00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:55.290
And that's actually
the easiest part.

00:17:55.290 --> 00:17:58.260
We just click Start
Training, and then we'll

00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:00.650
get a popup that says
something to the tune of,

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:02.530
all right, come
back in four hours

00:18:02.530 --> 00:18:03.953
and you can see your model.

00:18:03.953 --> 00:18:05.370
And then behind
the scenes, Google

00:18:05.370 --> 00:18:08.550
will train a model for us.

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:11.320
If we hop into this
Evaluation tab,

00:18:11.320 --> 00:18:12.930
we can see all sorts
of useful metrics

00:18:12.930 --> 00:18:15.060
about how accurate
our model was.

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:17.560
And, like I said, more
data means more accurate.

00:18:17.560 --> 00:18:19.620
So hopefully, over time
our model will improve.

00:18:22.370 --> 00:18:24.870
And then in the
Test and Use tab,

00:18:24.870 --> 00:18:26.520
we can actually
make predictions.

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:29.090
So I think I opened
this because I couldn't

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:31.790
write a menu on the fly.

00:18:31.790 --> 00:18:33.700
But I came up with
this little fake menu.

00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:36.200
I'll press Predict, and we'll
see if our model can correctly

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:39.580
identify the entities.

00:18:39.580 --> 00:18:42.570
And as you can see, it
identified all of the entities

00:18:42.570 --> 00:18:45.170
imperfectly because
that's machine learning,

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:48.510
and machine learning has an
element of probability to it.

00:18:48.510 --> 00:18:50.420
But if we were to
use this in our app,

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:53.330
it actually does save us a lot
of time inputting data, right?

00:18:53.330 --> 00:18:56.000
Because it identified
most of the foods.

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:59.070
It missed potato chips,
but it was pretty good.

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:01.340
And then as users
correct us in the app,

00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:05.300
we'll have more data
to retrain our model.

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:08.420
By the way, this is how we
use the app in the console.

00:19:08.420 --> 00:19:10.610
But you can also hit
it with a REST API

00:19:10.610 --> 00:19:13.170
or through a number
of client libraries.

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:13.670
All right.

00:19:13.670 --> 00:19:15.140
Let's hop back to the slides.

00:19:19.050 --> 00:19:21.810
I just showed you how to build
an entity extraction model.

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:23.700
But actually, there are
AutoMLs for all sorts

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:27.602
of different data types like
photos, video, and more.

00:19:27.602 --> 00:19:29.310
And if you want to
learn more about this,

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:32.610
make sure to check out my
session tomorrow at 12:30.

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:35.526
But with that, I'll
hand it back to Seth.

00:19:35.526 --> 00:19:38.514
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:42.627 --> 00:19:43.460
SETH VARGO: Awesome.

00:19:43.460 --> 00:19:45.050
Thank you, Dale.

00:19:45.050 --> 00:19:47.570
So far, we have showed you
how Google Cloud enables

00:19:47.570 --> 00:19:49.970
you to focus on your
code by deploying

00:19:49.970 --> 00:19:53.780
scalable back-end services using
Cloud Functions or Cloud Run.

00:19:53.780 --> 00:19:56.210
And Dale just showed us
how to add intelligence

00:19:56.210 --> 00:19:58.850
to our applications by
leveraging Google Cloud ML

00:19:58.850 --> 00:20:00.580
products like AutoML.

00:20:00.580 --> 00:20:02.510
And I have to say that
I'm an engineer that

00:20:02.510 --> 00:20:04.500
focuses on infrastructure
and operations.

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:07.048
And prior to this
talk, I had no idea

00:20:07.048 --> 00:20:08.840
about anything other
than machine learning,

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:10.190
besides Magic.

00:20:10.190 --> 00:20:12.387
And Dale has taught
me a lot about how

00:20:12.387 --> 00:20:13.970
we can leverage
different Google Cloud

00:20:13.970 --> 00:20:17.040
technologies without a
PhD in machine learning.

00:20:17.040 --> 00:20:19.370
And I feel like an expert now.

00:20:19.370 --> 00:20:22.620
I'm not, but I feel
like an expert.

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:25.380
So next let's look at how
Google Cloud can help us

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:29.600
with our builds, specifically
our mobile application builds.

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:32.600
So we have this Android
application code.

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:35.370
Again, this is Flutter,
but it could be anything.

00:20:35.370 --> 00:20:37.880
And we have these AI models.

00:20:37.880 --> 00:20:40.020
Now what?

00:20:40.020 --> 00:20:43.030
How do I get that in
the hands of my users?

00:20:43.030 --> 00:20:45.780
Now I could leverage something
like the Android Build Studio

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:47.910
locally on my MacBook.

00:20:47.910 --> 00:20:49.390
That would build my application.

00:20:49.390 --> 00:20:51.530
I could publish it to
the Google Play Store.

00:20:51.530 --> 00:20:55.420
But my local laptop is not
a hermetic environment.

00:20:55.420 --> 00:20:59.700
I might have packages or
users or configurations that

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:02.370
will cause my build to fail--

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:08.050
or worse, pass incorrectly by
linking to a system library

00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:10.000
or using the wrong
version of Java

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:13.300
or the wrong version of
the Android SDK or NDK.

00:21:13.300 --> 00:21:14.390
We don't want this.

00:21:14.390 --> 00:21:17.690
Especially if you have a
many different developers,

00:21:17.690 --> 00:21:20.690
especially across many
different organizational units,

00:21:20.690 --> 00:21:23.650
you want a consistent
central build environment,

00:21:23.650 --> 00:21:27.340
where you can control things
like versions, variables,

00:21:27.340 --> 00:21:30.070
substitutions,
packages, et cetera.

00:21:30.070 --> 00:21:32.670
And this is where Cloud
Build can come in.

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:36.600
Cloud Build enables us to
use the scale and elasticity

00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:39.270
of the cloud to
build and publish

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:41.310
our Android applications.

00:21:41.310 --> 00:21:45.450
With Cloud Build we can build
and test our applications

00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:48.060
in isolated hermetic
environments,

00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:51.520
leveraging the scale and
elasticity of the cloud.

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:54.330
Most importantly, we only
pay for the build time

00:21:54.330 --> 00:21:56.970
that we're using, and there's
a very generous free tier

00:21:56.970 --> 00:21:59.420
to get started.

00:21:59.420 --> 00:22:01.925
So with that, let's go ahead
and jump over to the laptop,

00:22:01.925 --> 00:22:03.550
and I'll show you
exactly what we mean.

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:10.600
So I'm going to go up here
to my build directory.

00:22:10.600 --> 00:22:13.290
So in here I have an
Android application

00:22:13.290 --> 00:22:14.970
that's built using Gradle.

00:22:14.970 --> 00:22:17.227
Now I totally could open
this up in Android Studio.

00:22:17.227 --> 00:22:18.060
I could click Build.

00:22:18.060 --> 00:22:19.268
I could deploy it to a phone.

00:22:19.268 --> 00:22:21.018
And I bet that many
of you in the audience

00:22:21.018 --> 00:22:22.480
have already done that before.

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:25.030
But how do I get this in
the hands of my users?

00:22:25.030 --> 00:22:27.630
Well, I could build it
locally using the Android

00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:29.350
Studio with a production build.

00:22:29.350 --> 00:22:31.290
But I want to leverage
the power of the cloud.

00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:34.260
For me to build this locally
takes about 10 to 15 minutes

00:22:34.260 --> 00:22:36.480
with my dual Core i7s.

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:40.680
But what if I could use, say,
32 cores to build this app?

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:42.432
That would hopefully
be very fast.

00:22:42.432 --> 00:22:43.890
But at the same
time, it guarantees

00:22:43.890 --> 00:22:45.420
that nothing on
my local laptop is

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:48.090
interfering with the
build, having it bit fail

00:22:48.090 --> 00:22:51.170
or pass improperly.

00:22:51.170 --> 00:22:54.250
So let me show you how we
submit our Android build

00:22:54.250 --> 00:22:55.600
to Cloud Build.

00:22:55.600 --> 00:22:57.070
Just like before,
I have a script,

00:22:57.070 --> 00:22:59.703
because I'm not good
at typing on stage.

00:22:59.703 --> 00:23:01.120
Let's jump down
to the very bottom

00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:03.460
where we have
gcloud build submit.

00:23:03.460 --> 00:23:05.560
We're running a build
by submitting all

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:06.893
of this code in this directory.

00:23:06.893 --> 00:23:09.310
All of these Android manifests
and [? Gradle ?] [? loop ?]

00:23:09.310 --> 00:23:12.640
files, we're sending all
of that up to Cloud Build.

00:23:12.640 --> 00:23:16.780
We're running our build
on a high CPU 32 core VM,

00:23:16.780 --> 00:23:19.480
and then we're passing
in some substitutions.

00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:21.310
What are these substitutions?

00:23:21.310 --> 00:23:23.950
Well, in order to
build an Android

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:26.410
application we need
the Android NDK--

00:23:26.410 --> 00:23:29.590
or we need the entered SDK,
and potentially the NDK.

00:23:29.590 --> 00:23:31.810
But every time I
build my application,

00:23:31.810 --> 00:23:34.910
I don't want to recompile
the entire Android SDK.

00:23:34.910 --> 00:23:36.910
I don't want to have to
download it, and install

00:23:36.910 --> 00:23:38.650
it and all of the dependencies.

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:40.900
With Cloud Build,
what I can do is

00:23:40.900 --> 00:23:44.510
I can cache those different
layers of my build steps.

00:23:44.510 --> 00:23:48.220
So I can download, say,
the Android SDK 27.

00:23:48.220 --> 00:23:50.320
I can cache that
as a build layer.

00:23:50.320 --> 00:23:53.050
And then when I'm ready
to deploy my application,

00:23:53.050 --> 00:23:56.920
I pull that build layer down,
and I just build against it.

00:23:56.920 --> 00:23:58.750
And then anyone else
in my organization

00:23:58.750 --> 00:24:00.520
that's building
Android applications

00:24:00.520 --> 00:24:04.000
builds against the same base
layer, the same base image.

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:05.680
And that guaranteed
some consistency

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:08.230
across our applications.

00:24:08.230 --> 00:24:11.890
I'm going to go ahead
and run this now.

00:24:11.890 --> 00:24:15.340
Now there are three machine
types for Cloud Build.

00:24:15.340 --> 00:24:18.210
[? We ?] have a single core,
an 8 core and, in this example,

00:24:18.210 --> 00:24:19.540
the 32 core.

00:24:19.540 --> 00:24:22.010
I ran this particular build
across all three instance

00:24:22.010 --> 00:24:22.510
types.

00:24:22.510 --> 00:24:25.300
It takes about 15 minutes
on the single core,

00:24:25.300 --> 00:24:26.830
about three minutes
on the 8 core,

00:24:26.830 --> 00:24:29.140
and about two minutes
on the 32 core, which

00:24:29.140 --> 00:24:30.817
is what we're using here today.

00:24:30.817 --> 00:24:32.650
But I don't think you
all want to stand here

00:24:32.650 --> 00:24:34.760
and watch logs stream
for two minutes.

00:24:34.760 --> 00:24:37.215
So let's go ahead and
jump back to the slides.

00:24:37.215 --> 00:24:38.590
We might come back
to this output

00:24:38.590 --> 00:24:39.700
if we have time in a bit.

00:24:42.550 --> 00:24:44.550
So let's recap
what just happened.

00:24:44.550 --> 00:24:48.060
I have an Android
application code.

00:24:48.060 --> 00:24:50.640
I submit that code
to Cloud Build.

00:24:50.640 --> 00:24:52.560
And Cloud Build can
build anything--

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:54.870
Ruby applications,
Python applications.

00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:57.480
Today we're building
an Android application.

00:24:57.480 --> 00:25:00.420
Cloud Build not only
compiles my application,

00:25:00.420 --> 00:25:03.840
but it also runs any
tests that I have for me.

00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:07.440
Then it uploads the
compiled APK file

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:10.380
to Google Cloud Storage,
which is our object store.

00:25:10.380 --> 00:25:13.350
From there, you can download it,
load it onto your test devices,

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:16.630
give it out to trusted
users for feedback.

00:25:16.630 --> 00:25:18.690
What we didn't do
in our example today

00:25:18.690 --> 00:25:21.510
is publish directly to
the Google Play Store.

00:25:21.510 --> 00:25:25.440
However, you could use
the Google Play Store

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:29.190
APIs to publish directly
to a beta or test channel

00:25:29.190 --> 00:25:31.020
on the Google Play Store.

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:32.730
And on the other
side, Cloud Build

00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:36.420
actually integrates with source
control like GitHub, and Git,

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:38.820
and supports these
concepts of build triggers.

00:25:38.820 --> 00:25:40.320
So you can imagine
a world where you

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:44.400
build a CI/CD pipeline for
your Android applications,

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:46.170
such that when you
merge a pull request

00:25:46.170 --> 00:25:49.320
or merge a change request,
within 10 to 15 minutes

00:25:49.320 --> 00:25:52.420
that change is available as
a beta build to your users,

00:25:52.420 --> 00:25:54.960
all in an automated
fashion, only paying

00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:56.560
while your build is running.

00:25:56.560 --> 00:26:00.160
This is truly leveraging
the power of cloud.

00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:01.660
But now what?

00:26:01.660 --> 00:26:03.100
We have our application.

00:26:03.100 --> 00:26:04.390
It's into production.

00:26:04.390 --> 00:26:06.010
Hopefully, we have some users.

00:26:06.010 --> 00:26:07.930
Right now, the gEats
app is really popular.

00:26:07.930 --> 00:26:09.030
There's two users.

00:26:09.030 --> 00:26:12.680
It's Dale and I. But now what?

00:26:12.680 --> 00:26:14.880
We're done, right?

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:15.380
No.

00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:18.000
We have to talk
about observability.

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:22.870
How do we gain insights and
metrics into our application?

00:26:22.870 --> 00:26:26.660
So let's come up with another
hypothetical scenario.

00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:29.520
Or sorry, let's talk
about observability first.

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:31.780
What is this thing
that I'm saying?

00:26:31.780 --> 00:26:32.850
I'm saying this word--

00:26:32.850 --> 00:26:34.890
"observability."

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:37.110
Observability is
an umbrella term

00:26:37.110 --> 00:26:41.580
that describes things like
monitoring, logging, alerting,

00:26:41.580 --> 00:26:44.770
tracing, dashboards,
and much more.

00:26:44.770 --> 00:26:48.090
It allows us to gain insights
into a single application,

00:26:48.090 --> 00:26:49.800
but also how our
applications are

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:53.580
communicating with one another.

00:26:53.580 --> 00:26:57.730
So now let's look at a
hypothetical situation.

00:26:57.730 --> 00:27:01.460
Let's say on social media
we get a message like this.

00:27:01.460 --> 00:27:04.490
"Hey, I really love your
new food delivery app.

00:27:04.490 --> 00:27:07.790
But sometimes it's
really, really slow

00:27:07.790 --> 00:27:09.800
to get my text message.

00:27:09.800 --> 00:27:13.040
Sometimes it takes nearly
five minutes to arrive,

00:27:13.040 --> 00:27:16.400
and I'm really hungry."

00:27:16.400 --> 00:27:20.430
Now as an application developer
who's generally internet savvy,

00:27:20.430 --> 00:27:23.230
I could quickly write this off.

00:27:23.230 --> 00:27:25.420
Clearly, they're
not in a good cell--

00:27:25.420 --> 00:27:27.640
they don't have good
cell phone reception.

00:27:27.640 --> 00:27:29.943
That could explain
the latency, right?

00:27:29.943 --> 00:27:32.110
Or maybe it's an issue with
their cellular provider,

00:27:32.110 --> 00:27:33.130
in general.

00:27:33.130 --> 00:27:35.770
Maybe it just takes their
cellular network provider time

00:27:35.770 --> 00:27:37.210
to deliver.

00:27:37.210 --> 00:27:39.820
Or maybe they have an old phone,
can't process the messages

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:41.330
fast enough.

00:27:41.330 --> 00:27:43.438
But what if I could
actually prove it?

00:27:43.438 --> 00:27:45.730
What if I could get a message
like this from my support

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:48.130
team or my social team,
and I could definitively

00:27:48.130 --> 00:27:50.140
go back to them and say, "Nope.

00:27:50.140 --> 00:27:52.060
This is definitely
not our problem.

00:27:52.060 --> 00:27:54.250
This relies somewhere
else outside of the chain

00:27:54.250 --> 00:27:56.380
that we don't control."

00:27:56.380 --> 00:27:58.630
And in order to do that, we
can leverage a tool called

00:27:58.630 --> 00:28:00.580
Google Cloud Stackdriver.

00:28:00.580 --> 00:28:03.370
Stackdriver is a tool
for observability.

00:28:03.370 --> 00:28:06.160
It provides integrated
logging, monitoring, alerting,

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:08.770
and tracing across
Google Cloud that

00:28:08.770 --> 00:28:11.320
allows us to gain better
insights into our application's

00:28:11.320 --> 00:28:13.060
performance.

00:28:13.060 --> 00:28:16.350
Let's go ahead and jump into the
autogenerated Stackdriver trace

00:28:16.350 --> 00:28:18.930
dashboard to confirm that
our text messages are

00:28:18.930 --> 00:28:20.185
being processed quickly.

00:28:20.185 --> 00:28:22.310
So let's go ahead and jump
over to the demo laptop.

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:29.050
Here I have the Google
Cloud trace dashboard

00:28:29.050 --> 00:28:30.520
in our web console.

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:33.730
And I'm particularly filtering
on an endpoint, which

00:28:33.730 --> 00:28:37.780
I've coincidentally named "slow"
for the purposes of this demo.

00:28:37.780 --> 00:28:40.222
Now I realize that some
of you may not understand

00:28:40.222 --> 00:28:41.180
what you're looking at.

00:28:41.180 --> 00:28:43.840
So give me a moment
to explain it.

00:28:43.840 --> 00:28:46.000
First, we're looking
at the past hour

00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:49.120
of traces, which is a measure
of the different steps

00:28:49.120 --> 00:28:53.330
that our code is taking
across our different services.

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:56.690
On the left- hand side, we're
looking at a data point,

00:28:56.690 --> 00:28:58.890
a sample of a data point.

00:28:58.890 --> 00:29:01.730
And on the y-axis,
it's the latency,

00:29:01.730 --> 00:29:05.100
or the total time it took
that request to fulfill.

00:29:05.100 --> 00:29:12.870
So a dot here, for example,
took 20,000 milliseconds.

00:29:12.870 --> 00:29:15.700
That's 20 human seconds.

00:29:15.700 --> 00:29:19.720
Whereas a dot down here
took 340 milliseconds.

00:29:19.720 --> 00:29:23.150
That's less than half
one human second.

00:29:23.150 --> 00:29:25.020
Now we can look at
this, and we can

00:29:25.020 --> 00:29:27.540
see that most of
our requests are

00:29:27.540 --> 00:29:30.340
down here at the very
bottom, which is good.

00:29:30.340 --> 00:29:32.880
It means they're being
processed very quickly.

00:29:32.880 --> 00:29:35.490
Ideally, we want low,
low, low latency.

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:37.170
So we can see that
most of our requests

00:29:37.170 --> 00:29:40.710
are being processed in about
300 to 400 milliseconds, which

00:29:40.710 --> 00:29:43.770
is really good considering
that's a mobile application

00:29:43.770 --> 00:29:47.370
going over the public internet,
invoking our endpoint, which

00:29:47.370 --> 00:29:49.410
is communicating with
a third party API,

00:29:49.410 --> 00:29:51.090
and then returning a response.

00:29:51.090 --> 00:29:53.070
So 500 milliseconds
for that round trip

00:29:53.070 --> 00:29:55.378
is really, really good.

00:29:55.378 --> 00:29:56.920
You'll notice that
we have a few here

00:29:56.920 --> 00:29:58.990
that are about 3 or 4 seconds.

00:29:58.990 --> 00:30:00.880
But those don't concern me.

00:30:00.880 --> 00:30:03.070
That could just be
network latency.

00:30:03.070 --> 00:30:06.520
But these ones up here,
that's concerning to me.

00:30:06.520 --> 00:30:08.830
Those are definite outliers.

00:30:08.830 --> 00:30:12.670
They're 20 times
and 40 times longer

00:30:12.670 --> 00:30:14.887
than any of our other traces.

00:30:14.887 --> 00:30:16.720
So let's take a look
at the difference here.

00:30:16.720 --> 00:30:20.110
Maybe we can gain some
insights into our code.

00:30:20.110 --> 00:30:22.560
If I look at one of these
traces that took about 300

00:30:22.560 --> 00:30:26.145
milliseconds-- scroll
down a little bit--

00:30:26.145 --> 00:30:29.710
you can see that our
endpoint was dispatched.

00:30:29.710 --> 00:30:33.220
Then about 5 milliseconds
later, 10 milliseconds later,

00:30:33.220 --> 00:30:35.595
it actually hit our code.

00:30:35.595 --> 00:30:37.220
And then about 3
milliseconds later, we

00:30:37.220 --> 00:30:39.860
dispatched our third party API.

00:30:39.860 --> 00:30:43.310
Very quick, very
fast, very expedient.

00:30:43.310 --> 00:30:47.950
Now let's take a look at one
of these outliers up here.

00:30:47.950 --> 00:30:50.860
Now we notice something
very interesting.

00:30:50.860 --> 00:30:53.707
Our request, again,
dispatched very quickly.

00:30:53.707 --> 00:30:55.540
There's only a few
milliseconds between when

00:30:55.540 --> 00:30:59.720
we hit our endpoint and when our
function was actually invoked.

00:30:59.720 --> 00:31:04.220
But then there's a huge block
of time, almost 20 seconds,

00:31:04.220 --> 00:31:07.580
until we call our
third party API.

00:31:07.580 --> 00:31:09.130
This is actually
an indication to me

00:31:09.130 --> 00:31:11.720
that our initial
hypothesis is wrong.

00:31:11.720 --> 00:31:13.580
This is a bug in our code.

00:31:13.580 --> 00:31:16.060
We're not waiting for
the third party API.

00:31:16.060 --> 00:31:18.470
We're not waiting
on network latency.

00:31:18.470 --> 00:31:20.560
This is all within
my application.

00:31:20.560 --> 00:31:27.550
And I know that it's happening
inside the SMS slow endpoint.

00:31:27.550 --> 00:31:29.710
This gives me immediate
insights into where

00:31:29.710 --> 00:31:34.225
to go to look in my code for
a bug or a performance issue.

00:31:34.225 --> 00:31:37.070
So let's take a look at that.

00:31:37.070 --> 00:31:40.730
And it turns out if we jump
down here to our slow endpoint,

00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:44.210
there's actually a
random 10% of the time

00:31:44.210 --> 00:31:46.175
we sleep for 20 seconds.

00:31:46.175 --> 00:31:48.050
And there's a really
embarrassing to-do here,

00:31:48.050 --> 00:31:49.565
that I was supposed
to remove this

00:31:49.565 --> 00:31:50.690
before going to production.

00:31:50.690 --> 00:31:53.870
And this was just
for local testing.

00:31:53.870 --> 00:31:55.995
And then we send a test message.

00:31:55.995 --> 00:31:57.370
And now I know no
one in the room

00:31:57.370 --> 00:31:59.828
has ever accidentally pushed
local test code to production.

00:31:59.828 --> 00:32:01.840
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:01.840 --> 00:32:04.530
But if you had,
hopefully it's clearer

00:32:04.530 --> 00:32:06.510
how a tool like
Stackdriver Trace

00:32:06.510 --> 00:32:10.197
made it easy and apparent
that this was our fault.

00:32:10.197 --> 00:32:12.280
And we can go to our support
team, and we can say,

00:32:12.280 --> 00:32:13.920
hey, your users are right.

00:32:13.920 --> 00:32:17.310
10% of the time, we were making
them wait 20 extra seconds.

00:32:17.310 --> 00:32:18.160
We fixed that.

00:32:18.160 --> 00:32:19.770
It's not going to happen again.

00:32:19.770 --> 00:32:22.500
And we built monitoring
and alerting around it

00:32:22.500 --> 00:32:25.776
so that we can proactively
detect it in the future.

00:32:25.776 --> 00:32:27.568
Let's go ahead and jump
back to the slides.

00:32:34.750 --> 00:32:38.700
So to recap, today we
discussed four areas

00:32:38.700 --> 00:32:41.760
in which Google Cloud Platform
can improve your developer

00:32:41.760 --> 00:32:43.080
experience.

00:32:43.080 --> 00:32:45.440
First, we talked about code.

00:32:45.440 --> 00:32:47.190
We talked about how
you can leverage tools

00:32:47.190 --> 00:32:49.290
like Cloud Functions
and Cloud Run

00:32:49.290 --> 00:32:52.590
to build scalable serverless
applications to support

00:32:52.590 --> 00:32:55.690
your mobile or desktop apps.

00:32:55.690 --> 00:32:59.260
Next, we talked about leveraging
Google Cloud artificial

00:32:59.260 --> 00:33:01.450
intelligence and machine
learning products

00:33:01.450 --> 00:33:03.940
to build intelligence
into your applications

00:33:03.940 --> 00:33:08.650
without needing a PhD in natural
language processing or machine

00:33:08.650 --> 00:33:10.130
learning.

00:33:10.130 --> 00:33:13.490
Then we showed how we can
leverage the power of the cloud

00:33:13.490 --> 00:33:17.720
to build our applications
on multicore VMs,

00:33:17.720 --> 00:33:21.350
leveraging elasticity in
a hermetic and isolated

00:33:21.350 --> 00:33:22.920
environment.

00:33:22.920 --> 00:33:26.070
And then lastly, once our
application was ready to go,

00:33:26.070 --> 00:33:29.180
we saw how we can diagnose
and analyze that application

00:33:29.180 --> 00:33:30.860
when it's in the
hands of our users

00:33:30.860 --> 00:33:36.200
by tracing our back-end services
leveraging Stackdriver Trace.

00:33:36.200 --> 00:33:39.322
This has all been in service
of one high-level goal.

00:33:39.322 --> 00:33:41.780
And if you're going to take
anything away from this session

00:33:41.780 --> 00:33:44.930
today, this is what Dale and
I want you to leave with,

00:33:44.930 --> 00:33:47.540
is that you should
focus on your code

00:33:47.540 --> 00:33:50.490
and let Cloud do
the heavy lifting.

00:33:50.490 --> 00:33:53.030
We are investing
resources in making sure

00:33:53.030 --> 00:33:56.763
that we are not getting in
the way of your core business.

00:33:56.763 --> 00:33:59.180
We want to support the business
with these different tools

00:33:59.180 --> 00:34:00.860
and technologies.

00:34:00.860 --> 00:34:02.450
Let Cloud do the heavy lifting.

00:34:05.120 --> 00:34:07.690
If any of this was
interesting to you,

00:34:07.690 --> 00:34:10.780
I'd like to invite you to
attend our other Cloud sessions.

00:34:10.780 --> 00:34:13.300
You can click on
Cloud in the app

00:34:13.300 --> 00:34:16.190
or on the mobile web interface.

00:34:16.190 --> 00:34:19.310
Specifically, I want to
highlight these four sessions.

00:34:19.310 --> 00:34:22.250
For a general overview of
many different Google Cloud

00:34:22.250 --> 00:34:27.510
Services, check us out on
stage 7 at 2:30 tomorrow.

00:34:27.510 --> 00:34:30.600
As Dale already said, she
has a talk later tomorrow

00:34:30.600 --> 00:34:33.420
about training a custom machine
learning model without any data

00:34:33.420 --> 00:34:36.159
science experience required.

00:34:36.159 --> 00:34:38.585
If you're concerned about
the security aspects of this,

00:34:38.585 --> 00:34:40.210
there's a really
great session tomorrow

00:34:40.210 --> 00:34:43.750
at 4:30 talking about how you
can secure your applications

00:34:43.750 --> 00:34:45.761
while leveraging Google Cloud.

00:34:45.761 --> 00:34:48.219
And if you are at all interested
in that serverless stuff--

00:34:48.219 --> 00:34:50.920
Google Cloud Functions,
Google Cloud Run, App Engine,

00:34:50.920 --> 00:34:52.360
App Engine Flex--

00:34:52.360 --> 00:34:54.639
there's a great session
tomorrow at 6:30

00:34:54.639 --> 00:34:56.230
on making the
right decisions for

00:34:56.230 --> 00:34:58.780
your serverless architecture.

00:34:58.780 --> 00:35:01.850
So with that, I'd like to thank
you all very much for coming.

00:35:01.850 --> 00:35:04.800
Dale and I will be happy to
take questions in the sandboxes

00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:07.840
down here on the stage or feel
free to tweet at us as well.

00:35:07.840 --> 00:35:08.940
Thank you all so much.

00:35:08.940 --> 00:35:11.990
